The  utility  manages  iSCSI instances on the local computer.  It
talks to the program to perform  this  management.   iSCSI  is  a
method  for  transferring  SCSI commands across a TCP connection.
The client which issues the SCSI command is called the initiator,
and the device which receives the command  and  takes  action  is
called the target; this mirrors SCSI devices, although instead of
being  physically  attached  to a host, the SCSI commands and re‐
sponses take place over a network.  iSCSI communication  is  done
in  sessions.  The iSCSI initiator logs in to a target across the
network, possibly authenticating itself; this  creates  an  iSCSI
between  initiator and target.  The initiator can then issue com‐
mands to and read responses from the target.  Firstly, the  iSCSI
initiator  on the local machine must be made aware of the network
location of the target.  The is used in to do this.  The  targets
can  be listed using the command.  To login from the initiator to
the target, the command is used; this creates a  session  between
the  initiator  and  target.  The sessions can be listed by using
the command.  Specify the directory where the socket  for  lives.
